Guard section 5 (issue #36 / ADR-0003): block the cron entry point in
front of PHP.

This file is a MARKER, not a configuration file. nginx never reads its
contents -- docker/nginx/default.conf tests only for its existence, per
request, in the directory docker-compose.yml bind-mounts over
/etc/wpcas/guards in the nginx container:

    location = /wp/wp-cron.php {
        if ( -f /etc/wpcas/guards/50-block-cron-entry-point.marker ) {
            return 403;
        }
        ...
    }

That existence test is what makes this section obey the same arming
concept as sections 1-4 (bin/guard copies an artefact into a destination;
presence is the only toggle, absence is the only off state) without
needing an nginx reload or restart: nginx re-evaluates the test on every
request, so `bin/guard arm 5` takes effect on the very next one, exactly
like a mu-plugin appearing in wp-content/mu-plugins does.

Why a marker rather than a conf snippet armed by being included: an
included snippet would only take effect on reload, which would make
section 5 the one section whose arming needs a second mechanism -- the
thing ADR-0003 rules out ("there is still exactly one arming concept
across all five sections").

WP-CLI is untouched by construction, not by a runtime check: this block
lives in the web server, and a `wp` process never traverses nginx or
PHP-FPM at all. Sections 1 and 3 need an explicit `PHP_SAPI` test to
exempt WP-CLI because they run inside the same PHP that serves both; this
one cannot see a CLI process even in principle.

What this section deliberately CANNOT do -- the trade-off it exists to
measure: nginx cannot read a PHP constant, so this block is
unconditional. It therefore cannot honour the fleet caveat the way guard
section 1 does (section 1 goes inert while DISABLE_WP_CRON is false, so a
site still depending on the web loopback does not silently lose cron).
Armed with the cron constant off, this section blocks the loopback anyway
and cron is genuinely lost -- see the
`nginx-cron-armed-cron-enabled` scenario, committed as that
counter-example rather than left as prose.
